  "notes": "Prices in USD per 1M tokens, standard (non-batch) API tier. Tiered-price models record the base (lowest) tier with the higher tier in the model's note field. Retired models excluded; deprecated-but-available models included. Collected Monday 2026-09-21, 7 days after the previous file. Tracked scope follows the set established on 2026-07-16 so week-over-week deltas stay meaningful. The set stays at 47 rows: nothing arrived, nothing left, and no row was replaced. Every one of the 47 rows is unchanged on input, output and cache-read price \u2014 a fourth consecutive week with no price change on the carried set, across all six providers, and the first of those four weeks in which the set is also identical in composition. One statement on the Anthropic page is recorded in this series for the first time, though it is not new to our coverage: Claude 4.7 and later models, and Claude Mythos Preview, use a newer tokenizer that the page says produces approximately 30% more tokens for the same text, with the exact increase depending on content and workload shape. Per-token prices are unchanged, so the eight affected rows (claude-opus-4-7, claude-opus-4-8, claude-opus-5, claude-sonnet-5, claude-fable-5, claude-fable-5-1, claude-mythos-5, claude-mythos-5-1) cost more per unit of text than a per-token comparison against a 4.6-and-earlier row shows. The pricing post has carried this caveat since it was first published on 2026-07-17; what is new is that the snapshot files now name the affected rows, so a consumer reading this series alone does not read those figures as directly comparable. This is a first recording, not a change dated to this week. Also newly recorded and outside the rows: OpenAI lists a cyber line, gpt-5.6-cyber and gpt-5.5-cyber, both at $12.50/$75.00 with cached input $1.25, and states that the aliases gpt-daybreak-blue-latest and gpt-daybreak-red-latest point at gpt-5.6-sol and gpt-5.6-cyber. That gpt-5.5-cyber pairs with a generation already outside the 2026-07-16 set suggests the line predates the tracked set rather than arriving this week, but the page carries no dates and this is inference, not an observation. Mistral added GLM 5.3 at $1.40/$4.40, labelled new and third-party, alongside the GLM 5.2 resale already carried as out of scope; the open scope question for \uae30\uc6c5 \u2014 whether a third-party model resold on a tracked provider's API belongs in this series \u2014 now covers two rows, not one. Still observed and still out of the rows, unchanged from 2026-09-14: Ministral 3 in three sizes (3B $0.10/$0.10, 8B $0.15/$0.15, 14B $0.20/$0.20), OpenAI gpt-5.2 and earlier, and Google's image, embedding, TTS, video, live and transcription models. Carried statements unchanged this week: DeepSeek still accepts the legacy ids deepseek-v4-flash and deepseek-v4-flash-vision-exp while serving both from DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash at Flash pricing; the three Gemini Flash rows at $0.75/$3.75 still carry a 2026-12-31 introductory expiry that doubles them on 2027-01-01; and Anthropic still states the Claude Sonnet 5 $2/$10 rate is standard and that the cancelled 2026-09-01 increase will not occur.",
